Ubuntu package builds for ONNX Runtime
======================================

This directory is an Ubuntu-oriented package recipe for the CPU shared library,
development files, Python bindings, and Python command-line tools. It remains
smaller than Debian's package because it does not build optional execution
providers.

Unlike Debian's archive package, this recipe lets ONNX Runtime's CMake project
fetch and statically link its pinned third-party dependencies. This avoids
backporting a large dependency stack to older Ubuntu releases, but it also means
the build requires network access and is not suitable for Debian or Ubuntu's
network-isolated official package builders. ThirdPartyNotices.txt is shipped in
the runtime package for the bundled dependency notices.

The GitHub Actions workflow builds in a container matching the target release.
In particular, the Ubuntu 20.04 binary must not be built directly on a 22.04
host: doing so can introduce glibc and libstdc++ symbol requirements that are
not present on 20.04. Ubuntu 20.04 uses ONNX Runtime 1.19.2, the last release
with upstream CPython 3.8 Linux packages. Ubuntu 22.04 uses 1.23.2, the last
release supporting Python 3.10. Ubuntu 24.04 uses 1.28.0 with Python 3.12.
These releases also fit the stock GCC versions in their respective Ubuntu
releases.

The Focal build applies `focal-eigen-download.patch`: it keeps 1.19.2's exact
Eigen commit but moves the download to Eigen's GitHub mirror because the bytes
generated by the original GitLab archive URL no longer match the hash pinned by
that release.

ONNX Runtime's Python package metadata requires newer NumPy (and, in newer
releases, protobuf) than some supported Ubuntu releases provide. The Debian
package deliberately builds against and depends on the distribution versions;
`debian/python-requirements.txt` records that version relaxation. The Python
autopkgtest exercises import, session creation, and NumPy-backed inference on
each target release.

FlatBuffers is used by optional Python tooling, but Ubuntu 20.04 does not ship
the `python3-flatbuffers` package. It is therefore a Recommends rather than a
hard dependency; the build workflow installs it on releases where it exists.

Debian's complete packaging metadata can be downloaded without its repacked
upstream source by fetching the debian.tar.xz member of the source package:

  curl -LO https://deb.debian.org/debian/pool/main/o/onnxruntime/onnxruntime_1.27.1+dfsg-1.debian.tar.xz
  tar -xf onnxruntime_1.27.1+dfsg-1.debian.tar.xz

Use `dget` on the matching `.dsc` instead when the complete, checksummed Debian
source package is required.
